CYNTHIA THOMAS: Hi,
this is Cynthia Thomas.

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.760
I'm a networking
specialist at Google Cloud.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:09.030
Welcome back to What's
New in Networking.

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:12.810
Today, we'll focus on edge
and security-related launches.

00:00:12.810 --> 00:00:13.560
Stay tuned.

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:16.857
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:23.780
First, let's talk about Cloud
Armor Managed Protection Plus.

00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:28.210
This is a service that packages
DDoS protection, Cloud Armor

00:00:28.210 --> 00:00:32.180
WAF functionality, and future
protection capabilities

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:35.750
into an enterprise-friendly
subscription.

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.870
Managed protection aims
to simplify Cloud Armor

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:42.320
deployments, reduce
management overhead,

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.420
and minimize financial risk.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:47.510
Cloud Armor Managed
Protection Plus subscribers

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:51.780
will also have access to
additional value-add services,

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:56.450
including adaptive
protection, named IP lists,

00:00:56.450 --> 00:00:58.940
and other future services.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:02.480
Well, the future is now, and
we have some exciting additions

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:05.600
to Cloud Armor Managed
Protection Plus.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.330
Introducing DDoS response
and bill protection services.

00:01:11.330 --> 00:01:14.090
With DDoS response,
customers can get help

00:01:14.090 --> 00:01:18.920
with analyzing and mitigating
ongoing DDoS attacks.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.160
Customers with premium
support have the opportunity

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:26.150
to engage DDoS response
services and get access

00:01:26.150 --> 00:01:30.860
to SRE and engineering teams
that are responsible for DoS

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:33.620
protection for all of Google.

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.710
Customers who suffered
from a DDoS attack

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:40.070
may see an increase in
their infrastructure bill.

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:43.200
With bill protection on
the covered resources,

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:46.340
you can now open a claim
with billing support

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.410
and receive credits
for the amount

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:53.450
of the spike, provided that the
attack was sufficiently large.

00:01:53.450 --> 00:01:57.110
Now, onto more
security launches.

00:01:57.110 --> 00:02:00.290
Until now, you couldn't
deploy firewall rules

00:02:00.290 --> 00:02:02.870
at an organization
or folder level.

00:02:02.870 --> 00:02:07.010
Now you can, with hierarchical
firewall policies.

00:02:07.010 --> 00:02:09.139
With hierarchical
firewall policies,

00:02:09.139 --> 00:02:11.690
security admins can
define and enforce

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:15.500
firewall rules at the
organization and folder levels

00:02:15.500 --> 00:02:18.650
in the GCP resource hierarchy.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:23.030
Firewall policies make
rules management easier.

00:02:23.030 --> 00:02:25.820
Policies contain
multiple firewall rules

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:30.710
that are attachable to an
organization or folder.

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:34.430
Hierarchical firewalls
enable automatic protection

00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:38.060
for new projects and networks.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:42.200
It also allows flexible
firewall configurations required

00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:44.880
for complex cloud environments.

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.980
Next is a super exciting feature
that just went GA, secure data

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:52.520
exchange for VPC
Service Controls.

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:56.540
VPC Service Controls is a
key differentiator for GCP

00:02:56.540 --> 00:03:00.380
and works to prevent
data exfiltration.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:02.930
With secure data
exchange, you now

00:03:02.930 --> 00:03:06.590
have more fine-grained
controls on the data exchanged,

00:03:06.590 --> 00:03:10.380
down to the method and
organization level.

00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.980
For example, you can
enable your partners

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:16.670
to have read-only
access to a GCS bucket

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.740
from a different organization.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:21.590
Finally, we're
excited to announce

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:25.610
the general availability
of request coalescing,

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:30.200
often known as
collapsing, for Cloud CDN.

00:03:30.200 --> 00:03:33.890
Request coalescing collapses
multiple user-driven cache flow

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:38.120
requests for the same cache
key into a single origin

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.270
request per edge node.

00:03:41.270 --> 00:03:44.390
We have already been applying
this behavior on purpose

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:49.220
since last year, but now, this
is the default for all request

00:03:49.220 --> 00:03:51.350
types.

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:54.410
Request coalescing is
now the default behavior

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:58.700
for all new and existing
Cloud CDN customers,

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:02.840
benefiting from a reduced
load on the origin.

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:04.610
And that's what's
new in networking

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:07.350
for edge and security.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:09.500
Let us know your thoughts
on these new features

00:04:09.500 --> 00:04:11.750
in the comments below.

00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:15.020
Check out the links for more
info on how to use them,

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:17.720
and join us next time to
learn more about what's

00:04:17.720 --> 00:04:18.470
new in networking.

